 {"id": "tb-mars-signal", "band": "trailblazers", "tags": ["space", "coding", "robots", "science"], "skill": "Problem solving and coding logic",
  "title": "{name} and the Signal from Mars", "pages": [
  ("Chapter 1. {name} had built a radio telescope out of an old satellite dish, a laptop and a lot of duct tape. Most nights it picked up nothing but static and the occasional weather report.", ("night", "grass", [("house", 70, 165, .9), ("kid", 190, 190, 1), ("satellite", 280, 170, 1), ("star", 300, 40, .5)])),
  ("But on Tuesday at 11:47 p.m., the static stopped. A pattern began: three short beeps, one long, three short. Then silence. Then the same pattern again, exactly fifteen seconds later.", None),
  ("{name} grabbed a notebook. Patterns that repeat aren't usually random. {He} timed the signal for an hour and pointed the dish in different directions. It was strongest when aimed at a small red dot in the sky: Mars.", ("night", "grass", [("satellite", 140, 170, 1.1), ("kid", 240, 190, 1), ("planet", 300, 60, .7), ("star", 60, 40, .4)])),
  ("{He} knew there was a rover on Mars called Wren, launched by a team at the space agency. Could it be Wren? But rovers send data to huge antennas, not homemade dishes in back gardens.", None),
  ("Chapter 2. {name} wrote down the beeps as ones and zeros: short meant 0, long meant 1. After ten minutes {he} had a long string: 0001000, 0001000, 0001000. In binary, 1000 is eight.", ("indoor", "floor", [("kid", 140, 190, 1), ("book", 220, 200, .8), ("lamp", 300, 160, .9), ("star", 60, 50, .3)])),
  ("Eight what? {name} searched the agency's public website. Wren had eight wheels. And a news story from last week said Wren had gone quiet after a dust storm. Nobody knew if it was broken.", None),
  ("{name}'s mum came in with cocoa. \"Still up?\" {name} showed her the notebook. She read it twice. \"This is either a big coincidence,\" she said slowly, \"or you've found something important.\"", ("indoor", "floor", [("kid", 120, 190, 1), ("kid", 220, 185, 1.1), ("cup", 300, 205, .7), ("lamp", 60, 160, .8)])),
  ("They wrote an email to the agency together. {name} attached the recordings and a careful explanation: what {he} heard, when, which direction, and what {he} thought it meant. Then they waited.", None),
  ("Chapter 3. Two days later, a video call. A woman with a badge that said Dr. Okafor, Mission Engineer, smiled at {name}. \"Your recordings are real,\" she said. \"Wren is calling for help, and our main antenna can't hear it.\"", ("indoor", "floor", [("kid", 130, 190, 1), ("robot", 240, 180, .9), ("planet", 300, 60, .6), ("book", 60, 205, .6)])),
  ("Wren's main transmitter was damaged by the storm. It was using a tiny backup radio, too weak for most antennas. But {name}'s dish, by luck, was tuned to exactly the right frequency.", None),
  ("\"We think Wren is sending a code,\" said Dr. Okafor. \"Eight might mean wheel eight is stuck. But we need the rest of the message. Can you keep listening?\" {name} nodded. \"Every night.\"", ("night", "grass", [("satellite", 150, 170, 1.1), ("kid", 250, 190, 1), ("star", 60, 50, .5), ("planet", 310, 60, .6)])),
  ("Chapter 4. Night after night, {name} recorded new patterns. Some repeated, some didn't. {He} built a little program to turn beeps into binary automatically and look for repeats.", None),
  ("The program found three messages. 1000 meant wheel eight. 0011 meant three. And 1111 kept appearing after them, like a full stop. {name} stared at the screen until {his} eyes ached.", ("indoor", "floor", [("kid", 160, 190, 1), ("robot", 260, 180, .7), ("lamp", 60, 160, .8), ("star", 300, 50, .3)])),
  ("{He} remembered something from the agency website: Wren's wheels were numbered, and each had three motors. Wheel eight, motor three. \"Wren is telling us exactly what's broken,\" {he} whispered.", None),
  ("Chapter 5. Dr. Okafor's team checked their models. If motor three on wheel eight was jammed, Wren couldn't turn left. That explained why it had been driving in slow circles since the storm.", ("space", "moon", [("robot", 170, 185, 1.2), ("planet", 300, 70, .8), ("star", 60, 40, .5), ("cloud", 90, 120, .6)])),
  ("\"We can send a command to reverse that motor and shake it loose,\" said Dr. Okafor. \"But Wren won't hear us unless we transmit at the moment its backup radio is listening.\" They needed a schedule.", None),
  ("{name} looked at {his} recordings. The signal always came fifteen seconds apart, and every 37 minutes there was a gap of exactly two minutes. \"Maybe that's when it listens,\" {he} said.", ("night", "grass", [("satellite", 140, 170, 1), ("kid", 230, 190, 1), ("star", 300, 40, .5), ("planet", 60, 60, .5)])),
  ("The engineers were impressed. \"That's a classic listen window,\" one said. They scheduled the command for the next gap: Thursday, 3:12 a.m. {name} was allowed to stay up and watch the mission live.", None),
  ("Chapter 6. At 3:12, the command flew across 200 million kilometres of space. It would take eleven minutes to arrive. {name} sat on the floor of the control room, hugging {his} knees.", ("indoor", "floor", [("kid", 120, 195, .9), ("kid", 200, 185, 1), ("kid", 280, 185, 1), ("lamp", 330, 160, .7)])),
  ("3:23. Silence. 3:30. Silence. Somebody coughed. Then a speaker crackled with three short beeps, one long and three short, but this time followed by 0000. Zero. Nothing broken.", None),
  ("The control room exploded with cheers. Dr. Okafor hugged {name}. \"Wheel eight is turning,\" she said. \"Wren is moving again, because you kept listening when everyone else heard only static.\"", ("indoor", "floor", [("kid", 110, 190, 1), ("kid", 200, 185, 1.1), ("robot", 290, 180, .8), ("star", 60, 50, .5), ("star", 320, 50, .5)])),
  ("A week later, Wren sent its first new photo: a red hillside, glowing at sunset. In the corner, scratched into the dust by its wheels, was a shape that looked a lot like the letter of {name}'s name.", None),
  ("Chapter 7. The engineers swore it was an accident. {name} didn't mind either way. {He} printed the photo and stuck it above {his} desk, next to the old satellite dish.", ("dusk", "moon", [("robot", 170, 185, 1), ("planet", 290, 70, .9), ("mountain", 80, 190, 1), ("star", 320, 40, .4)])),
  ("Every night after that, {name} still listened to the static. Because sometimes, {he} had learned, the most important messages are the ones nobody else is patient enough to hear. The end.", None),
 ]},
 {"id": "tb-whispering-woods", "band": "trailblazers", "tags": ["mystery", "nature", "animals", "detectives"], "skill": "Inference and evidence",
  "title": "{name} and the Secret of Whispering Woods", "pages": [
  ("Chapter 1. Everyone in Hollowmere knew the rule: never go into Whispering Woods after dark. Nobody remembered why. When {name}'s family moved into the cottage at the edge of the trees, {he} decided to find out.", ("dusk", "grass", [("house", 80, 165, 1), ("tree", 200, 160, 1.2), ("tree", 290, 165, 1), ("kid", 140, 195, .8)])),
  ("On the first night, {name} heard it: a soft whispering that rose and fell like breathing. It came from deep in the woods, and it sounded almost like words.", None),
  ("In the morning, {name} walked to the edge of the woods with a notebook, a torch and a magnifying glass. Detective rule number one: collect evidence before you decide anything.", ("day", "grass", [("kid", 130, 190, 1), ("tree", 220, 160, 1.2), ("tree", 300, 165, 1), ("bird", 80, 70, .7)])),
  ("{He} found three clues. Clue one: the whispering was loudest near an old oak with a split trunk. Clue two: the ground there was covered in tiny white feathers. Clue three: a smell of peppermint.", None),
  ("Chapter 2. At the village shop, {name} asked about the woods. The shopkeeper, Mr. Hale, went pale. \"My grandmother said the trees whisper to warn us,\" he said. \"Some things are better left alone.\"", ("day", "floor", [("kid", 140, 190, 1), ("kid", 230, 185, 1.1), ("house", 60, 165, .8), ("cup", 300, 205, .6)])),
  ("But Mr. Hale also sold peppermint tea, the only shop for miles that did. And {name} noticed his boots had flecks of white feather stuck to the laces. Interesting.", None),
  ("That afternoon, {name} met Priya, who lived on the farm next door. Priya had lived in Hollowmere her whole life and had never once gone into the woods. \"I'll come with you,\" she said, \"but only in daylight.\"", ("day", "grass", [("kid", 120, 190, 1), ("kid", 200, 190, 1), ("tree", 300, 160, 1.1), ("sun", 60, 45, .7)])),
  ("They followed the feathers to the split oak. Inside the hollow trunk was a wooden box. Inside the box: a bag of birdseed, a flask of peppermint tea and a notebook full of numbers.", None),
  ("Chapter 3. The notebook had columns of dates and counts: 12, 15, 9, 22. At the top of each page was the same word: OWLS. \"Someone is counting owls,\" said Priya. \"But why in secret?\"", ("day", "grass", [("chest", 170, 195, 1), ("kid", 90, 190, 1), ("kid", 270, 190, 1), ("owl", 200, 90, .8)])),
  ("{name} studied the numbers. They went up for years, then down sharply last spring. The last entry said: Only 4. Must protect the nest. Tell no one.", None),
  ("That night, {name} and Priya sat at the window with binoculars. At 9 p.m., a torch bobbed into the woods. Someone was going to the oak. The whispering began right after.", ("night", "grass", [("house", 90, 165, 1), ("tree", 230, 160, 1.2), ("moon", 300, 50, .8), ("star", 60, 40, .4)])),
  ("Chapter 4. The next evening, they waited by the oak before sunset, hidden behind a fallen log. As the light faded, footsteps crunched closer. It was Mr. Hale, carrying the flask of tea.", None),
  ("He sat beside the oak, poured some tea, and began to whistle softly: a breathy, rising, falling sound. The whispering! High above, two golden eyes blinked open in the dark.", ("night", "grass", [("tree", 180, 150, 1.4), ("owl", 190, 70, .9), ("kid", 300, 195, .8), ("moon", 60, 45, .7)])),
  ("An owl swooped down and landed on a branch near him. Mr. Hale scattered seed for the mice that the owls hunted. {name} stepped out from behind the log. \"You're protecting them,\" {he} said.", None),
  ("Chapter 5. Mr. Hale jumped, then sighed. \"These are barn owls,\" he said. \"Rare ones. My family has looked after them for a hundred years. The whispering is how we call them, like they call each other.\"", ("night", "grass", [("owl", 150, 120, 1), ("kid", 230, 190, 1), ("kid", 300, 190, .9), ("tree", 70, 160, 1)])),
  ("\"We told people the woods were dangerous so nobody would disturb the nests,\" he explained. \"But last spring, someone cut down three nesting trees. Now there are only four owls left.\"", None),
  ("{name} thought about the evidence. Keeping secrets had protected the owls for years, but it hadn't stopped the tree cutting. \"What if people knew?\" {he} asked. \"They might help instead.\"", ("day", "grass", [("kid", 130, 190, 1), ("tree", 230, 160, 1.1), ("owl", 250, 90, .7), ("book", 300, 205, .6)])),
  ("Mr. Hale looked doubtful. But Priya said, \"I've lived here my whole life and I never knew we had rare owls. I'd want to protect them if someone had told me.\"", None),
  ("Chapter 6. So they made a plan. {name} drew posters. Priya wrote facts about barn owls. Mr. Hale opened the shop for a meeting. Thirty villagers came, curious and a little nervous.", ("day", "floor", [("kid", 90, 190, 1), ("kid", 170, 185, 1.1), ("kid", 250, 190, 1), ("owl", 310, 120, .6)])),
  ("{name} explained the clues, the notebook and the numbers. \"The woods aren't dangerous,\" {he} said. \"They're in danger.\" The room went very quiet. Then an old farmer stood up. \"I'll help build nest boxes.\"", None),
  ("By autumn, the villagers had put up twenty nest boxes and made the woods a protected reserve. The schoolkids took turns counting owls with Mr. Hale, peppermint tea and all.", ("dusk", "grass", [("tree", 100, 160, 1.1), ("tree", 260, 160, 1.1), ("owl", 180, 80, .8), ("kid", 180, 195, .9)])),
  ("The next spring, the notebook had a new entry in {name}'s handwriting: 11 owls. Three new nests. Everyone knows now, and everyone helps.", None),
  ("Chapter 7. People still say the trees of Whispering Woods whisper after dark. Now, when they hear it, they smile, because they know it means the owls are home.", ("night", "grass", [("tree", 180, 150, 1.4), ("owl", 160, 80, .7), ("owl", 220, 100, .6), ("moon", 300, 45, .8)])),
  ("And {name} learned the best detective rule of all: the truth is most powerful when you share it with people who care. The end.", None),
 ]},
 {"id": "tb-championship", "band": "trailblazers", "tags": ["sports", "teamwork", "football"], "skill": "Resilience and teamwork",
  "title": "{name} and the Championship Comeback", "pages": [
  ("Chapter 1. The Riverside Rockets had not won a single match all season. They were the team other teams were happy to play. {name} had joined in September, and so far {he} had mostly practised picking the ball out of the net.", ("day", "grass", [("kid", 120, 190, 1), ("ball", 190, 205, .7), ("kid", 270, 190, 1), ("sun", 320, 40, .7)])),
  ("Then Coach Dee announced the end-of-season tournament. \"Eight teams. Knockout rounds. The winner gets the Silver Boot.\" Somebody laughed. \"We'll be knocked out by lunchtime.\"", None),
  ("{name} didn't laugh. That night, {he} watched videos of the Rockets' matches on {his} tablet. Not to feel bad, but to look for patterns. Detective work, football style.", ("indoor", "floor", [("kid", 150, 190, 1), ("ball", 230, 205, .6), ("lamp", 300, 160, .9), ("trophy", 60, 190, .7)])),
  ("{He} noticed three things. One: the team ran out of energy in the second half. Two: everyone crowded the ball like bees. Three: Theo, the quietest player, made almost every pass he tried.", None),
  ("Chapter 2. {name} showed Coach Dee a page of notes and diagrams. Coach read it twice. \"You noticed Theo?\" she said. \"Most people don't.\" She looked at {name} for a long moment. \"Want to help me plan training?\"", ("day", "grass", [("kid", 140, 190, 1), ("kid", 230, 185, 1.1), ("book", 300, 205, .6), ("ball", 70, 205, .6)])),
  ("The new plan was simple. Practise spreading out, like a net instead of a swarm. Build stamina with relay races. And pass the ball to Theo more, because Theo could see gaps nobody else could.", None),
  ("Not everyone liked the plan. Jaxon, the team's top scorer, folded his arms. \"I don't need to pass. I need the ball.\" {name} took a breath. \"You score more when Theo finds you in space. The numbers show it.\"", ("day", "grass", [("kid", 110, 190, 1), ("kid", 200, 190, 1), ("kid", 280, 190, 1), ("ball", 160, 210, .5)])),
  ("Jaxon looked at the notes. He didn't say anything, but at the next practice he passed to Theo twice. Both times, Theo slid the ball back to him and he scored.", None),
  ("Chapter 3. On tournament day, the Rockets faced the Hillcrest Hawks in the quarter-final. The Hawks scored in the first minute. {name}'s stomach dropped. Old habits pulled everyone toward the ball.", ("day", "grass", [("kid", 90, 190, 1), ("ball", 180, 200, .7), ("kid", 270, 190, 1), ("cloud", 300, 50, .8)])),
  ("\"Spread out!\" {name} called. \"Net, not swarm!\" Slowly, the team stretched across the pitch. Theo found Jaxon. Goal! One all. In the last minute, {name} intercepted a pass and set up Priya. Two one!", None),
  ("The Rockets had won a match for the first time all year. Parents on the sideline were cheering louder than anyone had ever heard. Even Coach Dee did a little dance.", ("day", "grass", [("kid", 100, 190, 1), ("kid", 180, 185, 1.1), ("kid", 260, 190, 1), ("star", 60, 50, .5), ("star", 310, 50, .5)])),
  ("Chapter 4. The semi-final was against the Northfield Foxes, the toughest defence in the league. Nobody had scored against them all season. At half-time it was still nil-nil, and everyone was exhausted.", None),
  ("In the huddle, Jaxon was frustrated. \"They mark me everywhere!\" {name} had an idea. \"If they're all watching you, someone else is free.\" Everyone turned to look at Theo, who went red.", ("day", "grass", [("kid", 120, 190, 1), ("kid", 190, 185, 1), ("kid", 260, 190, 1), ("ball", 190, 215, .5)])),
  ("In the second half, Jaxon ran wide, dragging three defenders with him. Theo, unmarked, strolled into the gap and chipped the ball over the keeper. The Rockets were in the final!", None),
  ("Chapter 5. The final was against the Eastbrook Eagles, last year's champions. By half-time, the Eagles were winning three-nil. In the changing room, nobody spoke. Somebody was crying.", ("dusk", "grass", [("kid", 120, 195, .9), ("kid", 200, 190, 1), ("kid", 280, 195, .9), ("cloud", 290, 50, 1)])),
  ("{name} felt it too, the heavy feeling of wanting to give up. But {he} remembered the notes from September. \"Three months ago we couldn't win any match,\" {he} said. \"We've already done the impossible twice today.\"", None),
  ("Coach Dee nodded. \"You don't have to win,\" she said. \"You just have to play like the team you've become.\" The Rockets stood up, one by one.", ("dusk", "grass", [("kid", 100, 190, 1), ("kid", 170, 185, 1.1), ("kid", 240, 190, 1), ("kid", 310, 190, .9)])),
  ("Chapter 6. The second half was different. The Rockets spread out, passed quickly and never stopped running. Priya scored. Then Jaxon. Then, with two minutes left, Theo made it three all.", None),
  ("Then disaster: the Eagles broke away. Only {name} stood between their striker and the goal. {He} remembered every hour of relay races, sprinted back and slid in. Clean tackle! The crowd roared.", ("day", "grass", [("kid", 140, 190, 1), ("ball", 200, 205, .7), ("kid", 260, 190, 1), ("sun", 320, 40, .6)])),
  ("The referee blew the whistle. Three all. Penalties! The Rockets took their kicks one by one. On the last one, Theo stepped up, quiet as always, and placed it in the corner.", None),
  ("The Riverside Rockets had won the Silver Boot. The team piled into a giant, muddy hug. Jaxon lifted Theo onto his shoulders. Coach Dee held the trophy high.", ("day", "grass", [("trophy", 180, 160, 1.2), ("kid", 90, 190, 1), ("kid", 270, 190, 1), ("star", 60, 50, .5), ("star", 310, 50, .5)])),
  ("At the celebration, Coach Dee asked {name} to say a few words. {He} thought for a moment. \"We didn't get better by wishing,\" {he} said. \"We got better by noticing, trying, and trusting each other.\"", None),
  ("Chapter 7. The Silver Boot now sits in a glass case at Riverside school. Underneath it is a small framed page of September notes, with three patterns circled in pencil.", ("indoor", "floor", [("trophy", 170, 175, 1.2), ("book", 260, 205, .7), ("kid", 80, 190, .9), ("lamp", 320, 160, .8)])),
  ("And whenever the Rockets lose a match now, and sometimes they still do, someone always says, \"Okay. What did we notice?\" The end.", None),
 ]},
